WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.141
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.441 --> 00:00:08.967
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:14.926
Unit eight: Legacy & Impact. Today you will project lasting achievements with the future perfect.

4
00:00:15.226 --> 00:00:17.844
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.882
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:22.717 --> 00:00:24.205
Enduring influence.

7
00:00:25.217 --> 00:00:29.364
Enduring influence means an effect that continues for a very long time.

8
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:33.575
The teacher's enduring influence is visible in her former students.

9
00:00:34.183 --> 00:00:36.940
Your turn. Say: Enduring influence.

10
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.637
Enduring influence.

11
00:00:42.383 --> 00:00:43.535
Transformative.

12
00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:48.024
Transformative means causing a major, fundamental change.

13
00:00:48.617 --> 00:00:52.427
Universal schooling had a transformative effect on the village.

14
00:00:53.017 --> 00:00:55.936
Your turn. Say: Transformative.

15
00:00:58.800 --> 00:00:59.929
Transformative.

16
00:01:00.683 --> 00:01:01.859
Contribution.

17
00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:05.999
Contribution means something given to help achieve a result.

18
00:01:06.583 --> 00:01:09.558
His contribution to Khmer linguistics is immense.

19
00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:12.791
Your turn. Say: Contribution.

20
00:01:15.633 --> 00:01:17.006
Contribution.

21
00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:18.809
Posterity.

22
00:01:19.817 --> 00:01:22.455
Posterity means all future generations.

23
00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:25.607
The temple carvings were made for posterity.

24
00:01:26.217 --> 00:01:28.811
Your turn. Say: Posterity.

25
00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:32.807
Posterity.

26
00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:34.586
Visionary.

27
00:01:35.583 --> 00:01:38.814
Visionary means having original ideas about the future.

28
00:01:39.417 --> 00:01:42.450
Her visionary plan anticipated the digital economy.

29
00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:45.679
Your turn. Say: Visionary.

30
00:01:48.533 --> 00:01:49.627
Visionary.

31
00:01:50.383 --> 00:01:51.907
Societal impact.

32
00:01:52.917 --> 00:01:57.041
Societal impact means the effect of an action on society as a whole.

33
00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:00.613
The societal impact of mobile phones has been profound.

34
00:02:01.217 --> 00:02:04.867
Your turn. Say: Societal impact.

35
00:02:07.733 --> 00:02:09.245
Societal impact.

36
00:02:09.983 --> 00:02:11.310
Lasting change.

37
00:02:12.317 --> 00:02:16.302
Lasting change means a transformation that persists over time.

38
00:02:16.917 --> 00:02:21.122
Real reform creates lasting change, not temporary headlines.

39
00:02:21.717 --> 00:02:24.741
Your turn. Say: Lasting change.

40
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.135
Lasting change.

41
00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:30.896
Stewardship.

42
00:02:31.883 --> 00:02:36.124
Stewardship means responsible management of something entrusted to one's care.

43
00:02:36.717 --> 00:02:40.423
Good stewardship of the forest benefits future generations.

44
00:02:41.017 --> 00:02:43.773
Your turn. Say: Stewardship.

45
00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:47.716
Stewardship.

46
00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:49.567
Zeitgeist.

47
00:02:50.583 --> 00:02:54.452
Zeitgeist means the defining spirit or mood of a particular period.

48
00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:59.279
The novel captured the optimistic zeitgeist of the 1960s.

49
00:02:59.883 --> 00:03:02.872
Your turn. Say: Zeitgeist.

50
00:03:05.733 --> 00:03:06.885
Zeitgeist.

51
00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:08.674
Heritage.

52
00:03:09.683 --> 00:03:13.633
Heritage means traditions, buildings and culture inherited from the past.

53
00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:17.248
Protecting Cambodia's heritage is a national priority.

54
00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:21.141
Your turn. Say: Heritage.

55
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.036
Heritage.

56
00:03:25.833 --> 00:03:29.473
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:30.317 --> 00:03:35.674
Visal, the founder of a rural library network is retiring next year. What will she have achieved?

58
00:03:36.783 --> 00:03:44.367
By the time she retires, she will have opened more than two hundred libraries and trained over a thousand volunteers.

59
00:03:45.483 --> 00:03:48.435
Impressive, but will the libraries survive without her?

60
00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:58.077
That's the real test of legacy. If she plans well, local committees will have taken full responsibility long before she leaves.

61
00:03:59.183 --> 00:04:00.579
And in twenty years?

62
00:04:01.683 --> 00:04:11.360
By then, a whole generation of readers will have grown up with access to books. That is lasting change, rather than a moment of fame.

63
00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:14.291
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:18.311
Grammar focus: Future perfect for legacy.

65
00:04:18.611 --> 00:04:19.832
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:20.517 --> 00:04:24.026
By the time she retires, she will have made a lasting change.

67
00:04:24.917 --> 00:04:29.041
By 2040, a generation will have grown up with books.

68
00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:33.761
Before she leaves, local committees will have taken responsibility.

69
00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:45.456
Use 'will have + past participle' for actions completed before a future point. After 'by the time' and 'before', use the present simple: By the time she retires (not 'will retire'), she will have…

70
00:04:46.217 --> 00:04:50.750
Be careful! Don't say: By the time she will retire, she'll…

71
00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:55.768
Say: By the time she retires, she will have…

72
00:04:56.068 --> 00:04:57.940
Present tense after 'by the time'.

73
00:04:58.733 --> 00:05:01.502
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:03.684
What Outlives Us.

75
00:05:03.984 --> 00:05:10.593
Legacy is often imagined as fame or monuments, yet the most enduring influence frequently takes quieter forms.

76
00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:17.593
Teachers, mentors and community builders shape lives in ways that will have multiplied long after their names are forgotten.

77
00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:27.577
Cambodia's temple builders created heritage that still defines national identity, while ordinary villagers preserved oral traditions across centuries of upheaval.

78
00:05:28.567 --> 00:05:35.164
Thinking about legacy encourages stewardship, since it requires us to consider how our choices will have affected posterity.

79
00:05:36.167 --> 00:05:43.309
Ultimately, a meaningful legacy depends less on what a person accumulates than on what continues to grow after they have gone.

80
00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:48.286
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:52.183
By 2030, we blank the new hospital.

82
00:05:52.483 --> 00:05:56.601
A, will finish. B, will have finished. C, have finished.

83
00:06:00.167 --> 00:06:02.491
The answer is B: will have finished.

84
00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:05.882
All future generations are blank.

85
00:06:06.182 --> 00:06:10.195
A, posterity. B, heritage. C, zeitgeist.

86
00:06:13.733 --> 00:06:15.733
The answer is A: posterity.

87
00:06:16.883 --> 00:06:20.404
Responsible management of something entrusted to you is blank.

88
00:06:20.704 --> 00:06:24.694
A, contribution. B, visionary. C, stewardship.

89
00:06:28.233 --> 00:06:30.210
The answer is C: stewardship.

90
00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:33.117
Choose the correct sentence.

91
00:06:33.417 --> 00:06:42.179
A, By the time he will arrive, we will have eaten.. B, By the time he arrives, we will have eaten.. C, By the time he arrives, we will eaten..

92
00:06:45.733 --> 00:06:49.254
The answer is B: By the time he arrives, we will have eaten..

93
00:06:50.417 --> 00:06:54.227
According to the reading, the most enduring influence often takes…

94
00:06:54.527 --> 00:06:59.261
A, quieter forms. B, the form of monuments. C, the form of money.

95
00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:05.310
The answer is A: quieter forms.

96
00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:09.675
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:10.517 --> 00:07:12.911
What kind of legacy would you like to leave?

98
00:07:29.717 --> 00:07:31.310
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:31.610 --> 00:08:05.187
I would like to leave a legacy of opportunity rather than possessions. By the end of my career, I hope I will have helped many young Cambodians, especially from rural areas, gain the education and confidence I was fortunate to receive. Monuments and wealth fade, but a student who becomes a teacher or doctor continues the chain of influence. If I succeed, my contribution will have multiplied through people I may never meet. That, to me, is the most meaningful form of legacy.

100
00:08:05.487 --> 00:08:07.081
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:07.983 --> 00:08:12.563
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and future perfect for legacy.

102
00:08:12.863 --> 00:08:17.686
Today's value is legacy. Live in a way that leaves the world better for those who follow.

103
00:08:17.986 --> 00:08:20.244
See you in Unit nine. Goodbye!

104
00:08:20.544 --> 00:08:23.800
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

